{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Privacy & GDPR Policy — Y Wal{% endblock %}
{% block og_title %}Privacy & GDPR Policy — Y Wal{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<section class="section page-hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h1 class="page-hero__h1">Privacy &amp; GDPR Policy</h1>
    <p class="page-hero__sub">Last updated {{ "now"|date("j F Y") }}</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--off">
  <div class="wrap news-post__wrap">
    <div class="news-post__body">

      <h3>Who we are</h3>
      <p>
        Y Wal (Cardigan Climbing) is a community climbing club based in Cardigan (Aberteifi), Ceredigion, Wales.
        For the purposes of UK data protection law (including the UK GDPR and the Data Protection Act 2018),
        Y Wal is the data controller for the personal information described in this policy.
      </p>

      <h3>Information we collect</h3>
      <p>When you create an account, book onto an event, complete an induction, or make a payment, we may collect:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your name, email address, phone number, and postal address</li>
        <li>Date of birth</li>
        <li>Emergency contact name and phone number</li>
        <li>Booking, attendance, and certification/induction records, including declarations you agree to and an electronic signature where relevant</li>
        <li>Payment records for donations or bookings, processed for us by Stripe (see "Who we share information with" below) — we do not store your card details ourselves</li>
        <li>Your mailing list preference (opted in or out)</li>
        <li>Notes our volunteers and staff keep about your membership, for club administration purposes</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you're recorded as the dependent of another member (for example, a family membership), some of this
        information may instead be held and managed by your parent's account.
      </p>

      <h3>How we use your information</h3>
      <p>We use your information to:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Create and manage your membership account</li>
        <li>Process and manage your event bookings</li>
        <li>Keep everyone safe — for example, by holding emergency contact details for sessions, and induction/certification records for restricted activities</li>
        <li>Process payments and donations, and keep the financial records we're legally required to keep</li>
        <li>Send you service emails relating to your account, bookings, and certifications</li>
        <li>Send you newsletter updates, but only if you've opted in — you can opt out at any time</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Our legal basis for processing</h3>
      <p>Depending on the activity, we rely on one or more of the following legal bases:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Contract</strong> — to provide the membership and booking services you've asked for</li>
        <li><strong>Legitimate interests</strong> — to run the club safely and effectively, including keeping emergency contact and induction records</li>
        <li><strong>Consent</strong> — for newsletter emails, which you can withdraw at any time</li>
        <li><strong>Legal obligation</strong> — to keep financial records for accounting and tax purposes</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Who we share information with</h3>
      <p>We don't sell your information. We share it only with the service providers who help us run the club:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Stripe</strong> — to process card payments and donations</li>
        <li><strong>Postmark</strong> — to deliver the emails we send you</li>
        <li>Our website hosting provider, who stores the data described in this policy on our behalf</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Some pages on this site also load fonts (and, for club administrators, icons) from third-party content
        delivery networks, which may see your IP address as part of that request.
      </p>

      <h3>How long we keep information</h3>
      <p>
        We keep your information for as long as your membership is active, and for a reasonable period afterwards
        in case you rejoin. Where a member leaves and asks us to remove their personal details, or where we no
        longer need to keep them, we archive the account: personal details such as email, address, and emergency
        contact information are permanently removed, while booking and payment records are kept in an anonymised
        form, since we have a legal obligation to retain financial records.
      </p>

      <h3>Your rights</h3>
      <p>Under UK data protection law, you have the right to:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Ask what personal information we hold about you, and get a copy of it</li>
        <li>Ask us to correct inaccurate information</li>
        <li>Ask us to erase your information, or restrict how we use it</li>
        <li>Object to processing based on legitimate interests</li>
        <li>Ask for your information in a portable format</li>
        <li>Withdraw consent at any time, for anything we process based on consent (such as the newsletter)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>To exercise any of these rights, get in touch using the details below.</p>

      <h3>Cookies</h3>
      <p>
        We only use strictly necessary cookies — for example, to keep you signed in. We don't use advertising or
        analytics cookies, and we don't track you across other websites.
      </p>

      <h3>Data protection contact</h3>
      <p>
        Our nominated Information Officer is <strong>Chris Tanner</strong>. If you have a question, concern, or
        request relating to your personal information, please contact us at
        <a href="mailto:cardiganclimbing@gmail.com">cardiganclimbing@gmail.com</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you're not satisfied with our response, you have the right to complain to the UK's data protection
        regulator, the <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Information Commissioner's Office (ICO)</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Changes to this policy</h3>
      <p>
        We may update this policy from time to time as the club or the law changes. The "last updated" date at the
        top of this page reflects the most recent revision.
      </p>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>
{% endblock %}
